00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.270 align:start position:0%
 
joining us now the chairman of the  commons health and social care committee  and of course former health secretary  himself jeremy hunt mr hunt lovely to  have you on the program i i wonder if we  might just start by giving an overview  of of the rep of the committee's  conclusions i mean it's it's been pretty  clear for some time that there needed to  be some reform of nhs litigation  thank you good morning neil um this  might seem a rather sort of technical  area um and it's very important to say  from the outset that the vast majority  of nhs care is is absolutely excellent  totally safe but when things do go wrong  we're very bad at learning from mistakes  and making sure the same thing doesn't  happen again in the future and one of  the reasons is the litigation system we  have which can mean that families have  to wait five six seven years before they  actually get an answer as to whether  they're going to get any compensation  it's absolutely devastating for them and  it costs the nhs over two billion pounds  every year to pay these claims  the biggest hospital in the nhs is  bart's trust  that's more than the entire annual cost  of paying for every single doctor and  nurse in bart's that we're paying in  compensation in litigation costs rather  than on frontline care and that's why  we're saying there needs to be an urgent  overhaul  what then in your minds needs to be done  i mean you're absolutely right to say  that you know lessons haven't been  learned in the past you only need to  look as far as the auckland report for  that so so what needs to be done and be  done pretty quickly  the first thing we say is that the  priority needs to be to learn from  mistakes  and to do that we need to make it much  easier for patients to get compensation  ironically if you make it easier for  people to get compensation you learn  from mistakes and it ends up costing you  less because these things don't then  happen again a few months or years later  and we looked at systems in new zealand  and in sweden which actually end up  costing their health systems about half  what it costs us here in the nhs and  that's because they they just make it  easier for people to access compensation  here you have to prove that a doctor or  nurse has been clinically negligent  before you get any compensation and what  that means is you have a very  adversarial process right from the start  the the medics feel very very defensive  they don't want to talk openly because  they're they're worried that there may  be professional consequences for them  and right from the outset you get a very  negative atmosphere when actually what  everyone wants to do is to be completely  transparent about what happened learn  the mistakes and make sure these  terrible tragedies aren't repeated  and this is the thing isn't it  ultimately at the core of this the nhs  held up by everyone but pretty much  everyone in this country  as one of the things that we are so most  proud of to be british it also has to be  it also has to be mentioned that  mistakes are made mistakes are  compounded by cover-ups i i just wonder  whether or not you think that the system  is hindered or it benefits from the kind  of the decentralized control of the  health service by giving control to kind  of the nhs trust is there more or less  of an attempt by by individual hospitals  to cover up their mistakes  well it's a very good question i'm  generally in favor of giving more  autonomy to hospitals and gp surgeries  just to get on with the job but i think  in this case the law gets in the way and  it makes it very difficult if someone  wants to i mean if you're a doctor or a  nurse and something goes wrong the first  thing you want to do is to be honest  with families about what happened  to be completely open about it  but your lawyers are telling you if you  do that uh you could be hauled in front  of the general medical council uh you  could lose your license to practice you  could cost us huge amounts of money and  there's one other thing that is really  outrageous in the law and really against  the founding principles of the nhs which  is that if the child of a banker  is severely disabled because of a  mistake they will get more compensation  than the child of a cleaner because  extraordinarily the way the law works is  it looks at the likely future earnings  of that child and it decides that the  child of the banker would have been  likely to earn more in the future that  to me goes right against the founding  principle of equality that underlines  the way the nhs works and we should  change that as well  um i wonder mr if we can kind of stay on  your brief such as it is but get you get  your kind of experience as a former  health sector and of course you know  when i'm going with this the high court  decision the high court uh judicial  review yesterday of matt hancock's  decision to move uh people elderly  people from hospitals uh into care homes  the prime minister said yesterday at  pmqs that the government wasn't told of  concerns about asymptomatic transmission  by public health england and that's  nonsense isn't it  well i think the first thing to say is  that the you know to be prime minister  or health secretary in a pandemic is an  incredibly challenging thing you're  having to make decisions very quickly  and you are going to make some mistakes  and i think we have to be reasonable and  think about the context that matt  hancock and boris johnson were operating  in  our select committee looked at this  report in detail and we concluded  similarly to yesterday's judgment that  we should have done much more to learn  from what happened in countries like  germany and hong kong  where when people were sent to care  homes for example in germany they had to  be isolated for three months unless  there was a negative test uh we didn't  do that here that was clearly a mistake  but i think the broader issue and it's  one that i have to bear my share of  responsibility for as well as someone  who has health secretary for six years  is that we just still have not sorted  out problems in the social care system  so when you have a crisis  it always ends up playing second fiddle  to the nhs the focus of if i may say  you'll also the media but the  politicians the civil servants the  system is on hospitals and hospital care  and that's what everyone is thinking  about  and  what happened  i would counter that we probably  wouldn't have known as quickly as we did  what was going on in our care homes if  it wasn't for the media including people  like our people in politics  correspondent nick martin getting in  there i mean the problem is i think i  suppose mr hunt is we can't deal with  this as an abstraction you know by june  of 2020 there were perhaps as many as 30  000 excess deaths and care homes and in  part that was as a result of a decision  taken by the health the then health  secretary seemingly unaware of advice  that if he'd switched on the today  program he'd have heard sir patrick  violence giving  yes i'm sorry i wasn't saying that the  media didn't have a very very important  role during that i was being somewhat  cheeky i was being somewhat cheap  but i think the the interesting thing is  actually if you look at the high court  judgment the advice the health secretary  received from officials  didn't advise him that he should think  about this question of asymptomatic  transmission so there was a kind of  group think in the system that said that  this  virus we were dealing with was going to  be essentially like the flu virus where  you don't think so much about  asymptomatic transmission i think there  are lots and lots of lessons to be  learned but the broader point i really  wanted to make is that until we really  fix the problems in the care system  then i think we're always going to find  the center of gravity in a crisis  remains hospitals and the nhs  and that in this case had devastating  consequences  mr hunt away from away from health  entirely and what do you make of the  reports that one of your parliamentary  colleagues uh one of your conservative  party colleagues i should actually say  um was seen by his colleagues watching  pornography on his mobile phone in the  house of commons chamber  well to be honest i just feel so  disappointed um i'm disappointed that it  it was someone in my party that was  alleged to have done this  but also disappointed for the house of  commons as an institution i have to say  my day-to-day experience as an mp is not  that this kind of thing is happening on  a regular basis but i just feel we  should not be putting female mps through  this kind of ordeal and it's totally and  utterly unacceptable and  clearly we have to have a very big  culture change and um you know and i  hope  if there is some good that comes out of  this it will prompt that culture change  that many people have been calling for  jeremy hunt always great to have you on  the program appreciate your time  thank you

